response:
Of course, this equals $\dfrac{\dbinom{9}{a_1} \dbinom{9}{a_2} \dbinom{9}{a_3}}{\dbinom{36}{3}}.$ Given a prime $p,$ of the three sums, there are $[{{9}\over{p^9}}]+1$ ways for $a_1.$ If there are $a$ multiples of $p$ and $b$ multiples of $p^2$ in $a_1,$ then there are $[{{9}\over{p^4}}]+b-a$ ways for $a_2.$ So we have $\displaystyle \sum_{\left(\substack{a+b\\=a_1}\right)}
 \dbinom{9}{a,b}\dbinom{9}{a,2a-b}2^a,$ and, multiplying to the 6th power, $\displaystyle \left(\sum_{\left(\substack{a+b\\=a_1}\right)}
 \dbinom{9}{a,b}\dbinom{9}{a,2a-b}2^{3a}\right)^2.$\endthink
answer$ =\displaystyle \sum_{\left(\substack{a+b\\=a_1}\right)}
